    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d571">

                <pb facs="0823" n="813" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg813"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">General Marshall to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-12-26">26 December 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>18. Appreciate very much any information and comments from you regarding Moscow
                        developments<anchor n="59" xml:id="d571-f1"/> which affect situation in
                    China. Especially interested in your present personal estimate of Soviet intent
                    in Manchuria and regarding Chinese Communists. Specifically I need all possible
                    assistance in evaluating whether difficulties the Generalissimo reports to me in
                    his relations with Soviet in Manchuria are purely in the pattern of our own
                    similar difficulties in Europe or whether Soviet policy here is deliberately
                    calculated to mitigate against an effective and unified China.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d571-f1" n="59" xml:id="d571-f1-content">
                <p>For documentation regarding the conference of the three Foreign Ministers at
                    Moscow, see <ref target="#pg829">pp. 829</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
